window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-10-27T11:46:22.733392Z","count":17,"pages":[{"title":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth?","url":"/ai-data-centre-electricity-demand/","description":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is driving a rapid increase in data centre electricity demand.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? Artificial intelligence (AI) is driving a rapid increase in data centre electricity demand. Artificial intelligence (AI) is driving a rapid increase in data centre electricity demand. Training powerful AI models, running everyday AI services and cooling the computer chips behind them all require substantial amounts of electricity. AI data centre electricity demand ai data centre electricity demand Updated Introduction The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that data centres consumed approximately 485 terawatt hours (TWh) of electricity worldwide in 2025. Its April 2026 outlook consumption of around 950 TWh by 2030, roughly doubling in five years. AI focused data centres are expected to grow considerably faster than the sector overall. IEA Although data centres still account for a relatively small proportion of global electricity consumption, their concentration in particular regions is creating significant challenges. Electricity availability, grid connections and infrastructure costs are increasingly shaping where AI facilities can be built and how quickly they can operate. How much electricity do AI data centres use? The scale of electricity demand becomes clearer when comparing global estimates with national figures. Measure Electricity demand Global data centres, 2024 Approximately 415 TWh Global data centres, 2025 Approximately 485 TWh Global data centres, 2030 Approximately 950 TWh projected Global electricity share, 2030 Around 3% projected US electricity share, 2030 9.5–15.3% projected Sources: IEA, Energy and AI (2025) and Key Questions on Energy and AI (2026); Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, United States Data Center Energy Usage Report: 2025 Update (2026). IEA A terawatt hour is one billion kilowatt hours (kWh). For perspective, a data centre using 100 megawatts (MW) continuously would consume approximately 876 million kWh, or 0.876 TWh, over a year. These estimates cover all data centres, not just AI. Traditional cloud computing, video streaming, business software, online storage and other digital services also contribute to electricity demand. Separating AI electricity consumption from other computing activities remains difficult because many facilities run mixed workloads. The IEA's 2026 assessment nevertheless that electricity consumption from AI focused data centres will triple between 2025 and 2030, even as total data centre demand approximately doubles. IEA This distinction matters. It would be misleading to attribute the entire data centre electricity footprint to AI, but equally misleading to ignore AI's growing contribution to new infrastructure demand. Illustrative overview Why does AI require so much electricity? AI electricity demand arises from several interconnected activities: training models, using those models, storing and moving data, and keeping the underlying hardware operating safely. Training and inference Training is the process of developing an AI model by processing large quantities of information and repeatedly adjusting its internal calculations. Large training runs can involve thousands of specialised computer chips operating together for extended periods. These chips, often graphics processing units (GPUs) or other AI accelerators, perform enormous numbers of mathematical calculations. They consume electricity directly and generate heat that must be removed. Inference is what happens when a trained model produces an answer, image, prediction or other output. A single interaction may require much less electricity than a major training run, but inference can occur millions or billions of times across a widely used service. As AI adoption increases, the cumulative electricity demand from inference becomes increasingly important. More complex reasoning, longer outputs, larger models and automated systems performing repeated tasks can also increase the amount of computation required. The IEA's 2025 analysis projected annual electricity consumption growth of approximately 30% for accelerated servers, which are strongly associated with AI, compared with 9% for conventional servers. Accelerated servers accounted for almost half the projected increase in global data centre electricity consumption between 2024 and 2030. IEA Cooling and supporting equipment The electricity consumed by AI chips is only part of a data centre's total demand. Facilities also need cooling equipment, pumps, fans, networking hardware, storage systems, power conversion equipment and systems that protect against electrical interruptions. Cooling is particularly important because high performance AI chips produce substantial amounts of heat in a relatively small space. Traditional air cooling can become less practical as the power consumption of individual server racks increases. Operators are therefore adopting liquid cooling, which transfers heat away from chips using circulating fluids. Depending on the design, this can reduce some cooling energy requirements, but pumps, heat exchangers and other supporting equipment still consume electricity. A 2026 IEA assessment found that AI server power density increased elevenfold between 2020 and 2025 and could rise a further fourfold by 2027. This places additional pressure on cooling systems, power distribution equipment and electrical infrastructure. IEA Illustration 1 Global Data Centre Electricity Projections Why is electricity demand growing faster than efficiency improvements? Computer chips and data centres have become substantially more efficient. Modern hardware can perform more calculations for each unit of electricity than earlier generations. However, efficiency improvements do not necessarily reduce total electricity consumption. If computing becomes cheaper and faster, companies may deploy more AI services, process larger workloads or use more sophisticated models. The resulting growth in demand can outweigh the electricity saved per calculation. This is sometimes described as a rebound effect: efficiency lowers the cost of using a resource, encouraging greater consumption. Historical evidence shows that efficiency can make a substantial difference. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory found that US data centre electricity consumption remained relatively stable during parts of the 2010s despite considerable growth in digital services. Improvements in server utilisation, cooling and the shift towards larger cloud facilities helped offset rising computing demand. LBL ETA Publications The present AI expansion is different because the number and power consumption of specialised servers are increasing rapidly. Google reports that its data centres achieved an average power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.09 in 2025. PUE measures total facility electricity use divided by the electricity consumed by computing equipment. A value of 1.09 means approximately 9% additional electricity was used for supporting infrastructure relative to computing electricity consumption. This indicates efficient facility operation, but does not establish that total electricity consumption is falling. A highly efficient data centre can still use more electricity each year if it contains more chips or processes more work. Google's figures are also company reported and should not be assumed to represent the entire industry. Google Data Centers The central question is therefore not simply whether AI chips become more efficient, but whether efficiency gains outpace growth in the amount of AI computation performed. Where is electricity demand putting the greatest pressure on grids? The global electricity share of data centres can appear relatively modest. Their local impact can be much larger. Data centres tend to cluster around established technology hubs, major network connections, cloud infrastructure and areas offering suitable land or tax incentives. This concentration can create major electricity demand in regions where transmission lines, substations and generation capacity are already heavily used. Ireland: data centres consume nearly a quarter of metered electricity Ireland provides one of the clearest examples of how data centre demand can reshape a national electricity system. According to the Central Statistics Office, data centres accounted for 23% of Ireland's metered electricity consumption in 2025, compared with 5% in 2015. Their annual electricity consumption reached approximately 7.66 TWh in 2025, an increase of 10% from the previous year. Consumption by other metered users grew by only 2%. Central Statistics Office These figures cover all data centres, not exclusively AI. Nevertheless, they illustrate the infrastructure pressures that can arise when computing facilities become a major electricity consuming industry. The Irish experience also demonstrates why national"},{"title":"Grid Pressure","url":"/grid-pressure/","description":"Ireland and the United States show how concentrated data centre growth can strain regional electricity networks despite modest global demand shares.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth?","level":2,"text":"Grid Pressure Where Is AI Putting Power Grids Under Pressure? AI Power Demand Grid Pressure Grid Pressure Where Is AI Putting Power Grids Under Pressure? Ireland and the United States show how concentrated data centre growth can strain regional electricity networks despite modest global demand shares. Ireland and the United States show how concentrated data centre growth can strain regional electricity networks despite modest global demand shares. AI data centre electricity demand historical comparative pillar population context Ireland's data centre electricity surge and what it reveals about concentrated demand United States data centre growth and the uncertainty in electricity forecasts Why grid connections, transmission capacity and substations constrain AI expansion ai data centre electricity demand grid pressure Updated Introduction AI data centres are putting electricity grids under pressure because large amounts of new demand are arriving in a relatively small number of locations, often faster than electricity networks can expand. Although data centres account for a modest share of electricity consumption worldwide, their impact can be much greater in technology hubs where many facilities compete for the same power supplies, substations and transmission lines. Ireland and the United States illustrate this problem particularly clearly. In Ireland, data centres accounted for 23% of metered electricity consumption in 2025, compared with just 5% in 2015. In the United States, rapid development around Northern Virginia and other technology centres is forcing electricity utilities to reconsider how much generation and grid infrastructure they need. The central issue is not simply whether a country can generate enough electricity. It is whether enough power can reach a particular location, at the required time, without undermining reliability or imposing unreasonable costs on other customers. Central Statistics Office Illustrative overview Why local electricity grids face more pressure than global figures suggest Data centres are not distributed evenly across countries. They tend to cluster near major cities, cloud computing hubs, high capacity fibre optic networks and existing data centre campuses. These locations offer reliable digital connections, established suppliers, specialist workers and proximity to customers. For technology companies, clustering can reduce operating costs and improve connectivity. For electricity networks, however, it can concentrate enormous demand in places that were never designed to accommodate so many large, continuously operating industrial facilities. A data centre requiring 100 megawatts (MW) of electricity can consume approximately 876 gigawatt hours (GWh) annually if it operates at full power throughout the year. That is 876 million kilowatt hours, before considering whether the facility's actual operating load is lower than its maximum connection capacity. A campus containing several such facilities can therefore represent a substantial new industrial electricity load. Unlike housing developments, where individual households consume relatively little electricity and demand varies considerably throughout the day, data centres can maintain high levels of consumption around the clock. This creates three distinct challenges. Electricity supply: Enough generation must be available to meet the additional demand, including during periods of low renewable output or high consumption elsewhere. Network capacity: Transmission lines, substations and local distribution networks must be capable of delivering the required electricity without exceeding safe operating limits. Connection timing: Grid infrastructure must be ready when a data centre begins operating, even though major network upgrades can take years to plan, approve and construct. The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that grid constraints could delay around 20% of global data centre capacity planned for construction by 2030. Its assessment also notes that building new transmission lines can take four to eight years in advanced economies, while delivery times for important equipment such as transformers and cables have increased substantially. IEA This helps explain an apparent contradiction: a country may have sufficient electricity generation in aggregate while a particular data centre cannot secure a connection. Electricity is not simply drawn from a national pool without physical restrictions. It must travel through infrastructure with finite capacity. A power station hundreds of miles away does not automatically solve congestion at a local substation. AI is making this existing problem more urgent. Large clusters of specialised chips can create unusually high electricity requirements, while demand for AI training and inference encourages operators to develop increasingly powerful facilities. Yet not every data centre serves AI workloads, and publicly available electricity statistics rarely separate AI from other computing activities precisely. Ireland's electricity surge: from 5% to 23% in a decade Ireland provides one of the clearest examples of how data centre expansion can reshape electricity demand in a relatively small national market. The country attracted major international technology companies partly because of its established digital industry, access to European markets and favourable conditions for multinational investment. Data centre development became concentrated around Dublin, where connectivity, existing facilities and supporting infrastructure were already established. As the sector expanded, electricity demand grew much faster than consumption elsewhere in the economy. According to Ireland's Central Statistics Office (CSO), data centres consumed approximately 1,240 GWh in 2015. By 2025, that figure had reached 7,663 GWh, more than six times the earlier level. Ireland: data centres'share of metered electricity consumption [cso.ie] cso.ie Source details in endnotes. 0% 6.25% 12.5% 18.75% 25% 2015 2017 2019 2021 2023 2024 2025 2015: Share of metered electricity 5% 2017: Share of metered electricity 7% 2019: Share of metered electricity 9% 2021: Share of metered electricity 14% 2023: Share of metered electricity 21% 2024: Share of metered electricity 22% [cso.ie] cso.ie Source details in endnotes. 2025: Share of metered electricity 23% [cso.ie] cso.ie Source details in endnotes. Source: Ireland's Central Statistics Office, Data Centres Metered Electricity Consumption 2025. Shares are rounded. Figures cover data centres generally, not AI alone. Central Statistics Office The most revealing comparison is not simply how much electricity data centres consumed, but how quickly their consumption increased relative to everyone else's. Between 2024 and 2025, Irish data centre electricity use grew by approximately 10%, while consumption among all other metered customers increased by only 2%. By 2025, data centres accounted for 23% of metered electricity consumption, compared with approximately 28% for all residential customers combined. Central Statistics Office These are shares of metered electricity, not shares of Ireland's entire energy consumption. Transport fuels, direct heating fuels and other energy uses are outside that comparison. Nevertheless, the figures demonstrate how one industry can become a major influence on electricity planning. Illustration 1 Ireland Data Centre Electricity Growth Why Dublin became a particular pressure point Ireland's challenge is intensified by geography. Much of its data centre development has been concentrated in the greater Dublin region, while electricity generation, including substantial wind resources, is distributed across other parts of the country. This creates a mismatch between where electricity is generated and where large amounts are needed. When numerous facilities seek connections in the same region, they can collectively exceed the spare capacity available in nearby substations and transmission infrastructure. Expanding the national electricity supply does not remove that local constraint unless the network can transport the additional power. Ireland's electricity regulator, the Commission for Regulation of Utilities (CRU), responded in November 2021 with restrictions on how system operators assessed new data centre connection applications. The criteria included the location of a proposed facility, its ability to provide electricity generation and its potential to reduce demand when required. The policy did not constitute a permanent nationwide ban on data centres. Instead, it made access to the grid conditional on the consequences for the electricity system, with constrained locations facing greater difficulty. CRU This marked an important change. Grid access could no longer be treated as a routine final stage of a commercial property development. Electricity availability had become a fundamental factor in deciding whether a project was viable. Ireland's 2025 policy:"},{"title":"Cooling Costs","url":"/cooling-costs/","description":"Powerful AI chips generate concentrated heat, making cooling systems and supporting equipment increasingly important to data centre electricity use.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth?","level":2,"text":"Cooling Costs Why Cooling AI Data Centres Takes So Much Power AI Power Demand Cooling Costs Cooling Costs Why Cooling AI Data Centres Takes So Much Power Powerful AI chips generate concentrated heat, making cooling systems and supporting equipment increasingly important to data centre electricity use. Powerful AI chips generate concentrated heat, making cooling systems and supporting equipment increasingly important to data centre electricity use. AI data centre electricity demand implementation pillar mechanism Why increasingly powerful AI server racks create difficult cooling demands Air cooling versus liquid cooling: electricity, equipment and water trade offs What power usage effectiveness reveals about data centre efficiency ai data centre electricity demand cooling costs Updated Introduction Liquid cooling is increasingly necessary for the most powerful AI servers, but it is not a complete solution to data centre electricity demand. It can remove concentrated heat more effectively than conventional air cooling, reduce some cooling overheads and allow more computing equipment to operate in a smaller space. However, it introduces additional equipment, installation costs and maintenance requirements, while the electricity consumed by the AI chips themselves remains substantial. The most important distinction is between making cooling more efficient and reducing total electricity consumption. A data centre can become more efficient while using more power overall if it installs additional AI servers. The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimates that cooling accounts for approximately 7% of electricity consumption in efficient hyperscale data centres, but more than 30% in some less efficient enterprise facilities. That difference shows why cooling design matters, but also why the potential savings depend heavily on the starting point. IEA For operators, electricity suppliers and local communities, the practical comparison is: Cooling approach Main advantage Main limitation Conventional air cooling Established equipment, relatively straightforward maintenance Increasingly difficult for densely packed, high power AI racks Direct to chip liquid cooling Removes heat close to powerful processors and supports higher rack densities Requires liquid distribution equipment, compatible servers and careful maintenance Immersion cooling Can remove heat from densely packed components using specialised fluids Requires substantial changes to equipment, servicing and infrastructure Evaporative cooling Can reduce electricity needed for mechanical refrigeration Consumes water, sometimes in areas already experiencing water stress Dry or closed loop heat rejection Can substantially reduce direct water consumption May increase electricity demand, especially during hot weather These technologies are not always alternatives. A facility may use liquid cooling inside server racks, air cooling for other equipment and evaporative or dry cooling to release heat outdoors. The central question is therefore not simply which cooling technology uses the least electricity. It is which combination delivers reliable computing capacity at an acceptable total cost in electricity, water, equipment and local infrastructure. Illustrative overview Why powerful AI racks are harder to cool AI chips turn most of the electricity they consume into heat. That heat must be removed continuously to prevent processors from overheating, slowing down or shutting down. Conventional servers already generate significant heat, but modern AI systems concentrate much more computing power into individual racks. A rack is a cabinet containing multiple servers, network connections and supporting equipment. The problem is not merely that an AI data centre generates more heat overall. It is that increasingly large quantities of heat are produced within a relatively small physical area. Heat density changes the engineering problem Traditional data centre cooling relies heavily on moving cold air through server rooms. Fans push air across hot components, while air conditioning equipment removes the heat. This approach becomes harder as rack power increases. More electricity flowing through a rack means more heat must be transferred away from its components. At sufficiently high densities, the required airflow becomes difficult to deliver efficiently. NVIDIA's GB200 NVL72 illustrates the change. The system combines 72 graphics processing units (GPUs) and 36 central processing units (CPUs) in a tightly integrated, liquid cooled architecture. It is designed to operate as a connected computing system rather than as a collection of relatively independent servers. NVIDIA This arrangement helps AI processors exchange information rapidly, but also concentrates their electricity demand and heat output. The engineering consequences extend beyond the chips: Airflow: Moving enough air through densely packed equipment can require larger fans, greater pressure and redesigned airflow paths. Cooling capacity: The facility must remove heat continuously, including during periods of maximum AI workload. Power distribution: Electrical equipment must support high rack loads, while power conversion losses generate additional heat. Reliability: Cooling failures can affect expensive computing equipment and interrupt AI services. Building design: Rack layout, pipework, floor loading and available space increasingly influence which AI systems a facility can accommodate. Research published by Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory in 2026 highlights the importance of this shift towards high power density infrastructure. It also notes that improved liquid cooling can increase the temperature at which waste heat is collected, potentially making that heat more useful elsewhere. Energy Analysis Division Why more efficient chips do not eliminate heat A more efficient AI processor can perform more calculations for each unit of electricity consumed. That is a genuine improvement, particularly when comparing the energy required to complete a specific task. However, efficiency per calculation and total power consumption are different measurements. If newer processors deliver substantially more computing capacity, operators may install larger clusters or run more demanding workloads. Electricity demand can consequently increase even when the energy required for individual calculations decreases. Cooling equipment must handle the electricity actually consumed, not the theoretical efficiency of the processors. For example, a cluster drawing 10 megawatts (MW) of computing power will produce roughly 10 MW of heat from that equipment, regardless of how many AI calculations it completes. A more efficient chip only reduces the heat burden if it reduces electricity consumption for the workload being delivered. This is one reason improvements in chip efficiency cannot automatically be translated into equivalent reductions in data centre electricity demand. Air cooling versus liquid cooling The most important difference between air and liquid cooling is how effectively they transfer heat away from electronic components. Air cooling moves large volumes of air across equipment. Liquid cooling uses a circulating fluid to collect heat more directly, taking advantage of liquids'much greater capacity to carry heat. For high density AI systems, liquid cooling can therefore provide a practical route to operating hardware that would otherwise be difficult or expensive to cool using air alone. Illustration 1 Cooling Methods for AI Data Centres Direct to chip cooling Direct to chip cooling circulates liquid through specially designed metal plates attached to processors and other high heat components. Heat passes from the chip into the plate and then into the circulating liquid. The warmed liquid travels to a cooling distribution unit, which transfers heat into another cooling circuit. The fluid generally remains separated from the electronics. This design can reduce reliance on high volume airflow and allow the facility to operate with warmer cooling water than would be practical in some conventional air cooled systems. Warmer cooling water matters because it can reduce the need for energy intensive refrigeration. Under suitable outdoor conditions, a facility may be able to reject heat without operating conventional chillers as heavily. However, direct to chip cooling rarely removes every source of heat inside a server. Memory, power supplies, networking components and other equipment may still need air cooling. Consequently, many AI facilities use hybrid systems combining liquid cooled processors with conventional air cooled components. Immersion cooling Immersion cooling takes a different approach. Electronic components are placed in a specialised electrically insulating liquid that absorbs their heat. Some systems circulate the liquid through heat exchangers. Others use fluids that boil at relatively low temperatures and condense again within a"},{"title":"Who Pays","url":"/who-pays/","description":"Expanding AI data centres can require costly electricity infrastructure, raising questions about who funds upgrades and who bears the financial risks.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth?","level":2,"text":"Who Pays Could AI Data Centres Push Up Electricity Bills? AI Power Demand Who Pays Who Pays Could AI Data Centres Push Up Electricity Bills? Expanding AI data centres can require costly electricity infrastructure, raising questions about who funds upgrades and who bears the financial risks. Expanding AI data centres can require costly electricity infrastructure, raising questions about who funds upgrades and who bears the financial risks. AI data centre electricity demand governance pillar policy intervention The cost of supplying AI data centres with new generation and grid infrastructure How infrastructure spending can affect technology companies, electricity customers and taxpayers How grid planning, connection charges and uncertain demand forecasts shape who pays ai data centre electricity demand who pays Updated Introduction AI data centres could push up electricity bills, but the outcome depends heavily on who is required to pay for the power stations, cables, substations and grid upgrades needed to serve them. Technology companies normally pay for the electricity they consume and may fund their own connections or power supplies. However, wider electricity infrastructure is often financed by regulated network companies and recovered through charges paid by households and businesses. The central question is whether AI companies pay the additional costs their facilities create, or whether some of those costs are spread across other electricity customers and taxpayers. This is becoming a significant policy issue. The International Energy Agency (IEA) expects global data centre electricity consumption to rise from approximately 485 terawatt hours (TWh) in 2025 to 950 TWh in 2030, with AI focused facilities growing particularly quickly. The expansion is creating demand for infrastructure that can take years to build and may remain in service for decades. IEA The financial consequences depend on decisions made before a data centre opens: which infrastructure is approved, who finances construction, how costs are allocated through electricity tariffs, and what happens if projected AI demand fails to materialise. What electricity infrastructure does AI actually require? A large AI data centre needs more than a connection to an existing electricity cable. It may require substantial new generating capacity, high voltage transmission infrastructure, local substations and equipment to keep the electricity supply stable. These investments serve different purposes, and the distinction matters because their costs are recovered in different ways. New electricity generation provides the additional power needed to run servers, cooling equipment and supporting systems. This may come from new renewable projects, gas fired power stations, nuclear generation or existing power plants operating more intensively. Transmission infrastructure moves large quantities of electricity across regions. New high voltage lines may be necessary when data centres are concentrated far from existing generating capacity or where transmission networks are already congested. Distribution and connection infrastructure delivers electricity to individual sites. Large facilities may need dedicated substations, transformers, switching equipment and new cables. Some connect directly to transmission networks rather than ordinary local distribution networks. System wide infrastructure maintains reliability. Electricity networks must have sufficient capacity to manage demand peaks, equipment failures and unexpected interruptions. The need for reserve capacity, storage or network reinforcement can create costs beyond the equipment physically connected to a data centre. The IEA's World Energy Investment 2025 estimated that meeting growing data centre electricity demand would require more than US$170 billion of investment in new electricity generation capacity globally by 2030. This is a generation investment estimate, not a complete bill for every transmission line, substation or local connection. 10th Edition Illustrative overview Why electricity infrastructure can cost more than expected A data centre operator may budget for a particular electricity connection, only to discover that the local grid cannot supply the requested power without substantial reinforcement. For example, connecting a 200 megawatt (MW) facility may involve upgrading a nearby substation. But if the wider transmission network cannot reliably deliver another 200 MW to the area, additional work may be needed much further away. The resulting costs may be distributed across several infrastructure and electricity customers, rather than appearing as one identifiable charge to the data centre developer. Timing adds another difficulty. The IEA's 2025 Energy and AI assessment found that new transmission lines can take four to eight years to develop in advanced economies. It also estimated that grid constraints could delay around 20% of planned global data centre capacity by 2030. IEA This creates pressure to invest ahead of confirmed demand. Waiting until every AI project is operational risks electricity shortages and lengthy connection delays. Building too early, however, risks leaving customers paying for infrastructure that turns out to be unnecessary. Who pays for the new power stations and grid upgrades? There is no universal rule requiring technology companies to pay every cost associated with their electricity demand. Payment depends on the ownership of the infrastructure, electricity market rules, connection agreements and decisions by regulators. In practice, several groups can contribute to the same project. Who pays? Typical financial responsibility Main risk Data centre developers and technology companies Site connections, electricity purchases, dedicated equipment and contracted generation Paying for capacity that is no longer needed Electricity network companies Financing approved transmission and distribution upgrades Construction costs and investment recovery Households and other electricity customers Network charges and some shared system costs Higher bills or costs from unused infrastructure Power generators and investors Construction and financing of new generating capacity Electricity sales or contracts failing to cover investment Governments and taxpayers Subsidies, tax incentives, public financing or guarantees Public spending without sufficient economic return These categories overlap. A network company might finance a new substation initially, recover part of its cost from the data centre through connection charges, and recover another part from electricity users over many years. Technology companies: direct payments and long term contracts Major technology companies can finance their electricity needs in several ways. They may pay directly for dedicated connection assets, enter long term electricity supply agreements or invest in power generation. A power purchase agreement (PPA) is a contract under which a company agrees to buy electricity from a generator, often for several years. Such agreements can help developers finance new renewable or other generating because they provide greater certainty about future revenue. The IEA reported that the technology sector accounted for around 40% of corporate renewable power purchase agreements signed in 2025. Technology companies are also supporting investment in nuclear and advanced geothermal electricity generation. IEA However, signing a power purchase agreement does not necessarily mean a technology company has paid for all the infrastructure needed to deliver that electricity. A wind farm, for example, may require new transmission capacity. A data centre buying its electricity may still depend on network upgrades financed through charges collected from a much wider group of customers. Likewise, buying enough renewable electricity over a year does not automatically guarantee that the data centre can receive carbon free electricity during every hour it operates. Other generators, storage or grid services may still be needed. The important distinction is between paying for electricity and paying the full additional cost of making that electricity reliably available at a particular location. Electricity network companies: financing now, recovering costs later Electricity transmission and distribution networks are expensive assets with long operating lives. Their owners typically finance construction through borrowing and investment capital, then recover approved expenditure through regulated charges. In Great Britain, the energy regulator Ofgem controls how much revenue regulated network companies can collect through its RIIO framework. This is intended to allow necessary investment while protecting consumers from excessive charges. Ofgem's electricity transmission price control for April 2026 to March 2031 includes provision for major network investment, with further funding dependent on assessed system needs and costs. Ofgem This arrangement"},{"title":"AI Energy Use","url":"/ai-energy-use/","description":"Training AI models requires powerful computing clusters, while billions of everyday AI requests can create substantial ongoing electricity demand.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth?","level":2,"text":"AI Energy Use How Much Electricity Does Running AI Actually Use? AI Power Demand AI Energy Use AI Energy Use How Much Electricity Does Running AI Actually Use? Training AI models requires powerful computing clusters, while billions of everyday AI requests can create substantial ongoing electricity demand. Training AI models requires powerful computing clusters, while billions of everyday AI requests can create substantial ongoing electricity demand. AI data centre electricity demand mechanism pillar mechanism Training versus inference: where the electricity goes How AI model size, reasoning and repeated requests change energy consumption Why more efficient AI chips may not reduce total electricity demand ai data centre electricity demand ai energy use Updated Introduction AI electricity demand is driven by two different activities: training models and using them. Training requires large clusters of specialised chips working intensively for weeks or months. Inference —the process of generating an answer, image or prediction from a trained model —usually consumes far less electricity per task, but can happen billions of times. The key distinction is between the electricity needed to build an AI model and the ongoing electricity needed to operate it at scale. Neither can be judged in isolation. A model that is expensive to train may become relatively efficient over millions of uses, while a cheap individual AI request can contribute to substantial electricity demand when repeated across a global service. Research published in 2026 suggests that an ordinary text request to an efficiently operated AI system can consume less than one watt hour. More demanding reasoning tasks can use over ten times as much. Meanwhile, the International Energy Agency (IEA) that electricity consumption from AI focused data centres will triple between 2025 and 2030. ScienceDirect For readers assessing AI's energy costs, three comparisons matter most: Training versus inference: Training creates large, concentrated electricity loads; inference creates continuing demand that grows with adoption. Simple versus complex requests: Short text responses can be relatively efficient, while long reasoning tasks and automated workflows require substantially more computation. Efficiency versus total consumption: Better chips can reduce electricity per calculation without reducing total demand if companies perform many more calculations. Understanding these differences is essential for judging whether AI electricity forecasts are realistic, which infrastructure investments are necessary and where the resulting costs fall. Illustrative overview Training versus inference: where does the electricity go? Training and inference use similar underlying hardware, but their electricity demand follows different patterns. Factor AI training AI inference Main purpose Develop or improve a model Produce outputs using a trained model Typical workload Repeated calculations across large datasets Processing prompts and generating responses Electricity pattern Intensive, often prolonged computing runs Continuous, variable demand from users and applications Main driver Training computation, chip numbers and run duration Request volume, model choice and computation per request Main infrastructure challenge Supplying enough power to large computing clusters Serving large volumes of requests efficiently and reliably Main uncertainty Future training scale and frequency Future adoption and complexity of AI use These differences matter because a data centre built primarily for training can face different power requirements from one designed to serve millions of users throughout the day. Training: concentrated electricity consumption Training a large AI model involves processing enormous quantities of data and repeatedly adjusting billions or trillions of numerical values known as parameters. These parameters determine how the model processes information and generates outputs. The calculations are carried out by specialised processors, including graphics processing units (GPUs) and tensor processing units (TPUs). Large training systems connect thousands of these chips through high speed networks so that they can work together. The electricity required depends on several interacting factors: the number and type of chips, their power consumption, the duration of the training run, how effectively the hardware is utilised and the electricity needed for supporting equipment. A simple calculation illustrates the scale. Illustrative training workload Average computing load Duration Electricity consumed by the computing load 50 MW × 24 hours × 60 days = 72,000 MWh. This is an illustrative calculation, not a measurement of a particular model. Cooling and other data centre overheads would increase the facility total. A gigawatt hour (GWh) is one million kilowatt hours. The example shows why even a single major training run can require substantial electricity, despite occupying a relatively short period compared with a data centre's operating life. Research organisation Epoch AI estimated that training Google's Gemini Ultra model required approximately 35 megawatts of electrical power. Its 2024 analysis also found that electricity accounted for approximately 2–6% of the estimated development costs of selected frontier models, while computing hardware and research staff accounted for much larger shares. Epoch AI This reveals an important distinction between financial and physical costs. Electricity may represent a relatively small share of an AI company's overall model development expenditure, yet securing tens or hundreds of megawatts of power can still determine whether a training project is feasible. Training also extends beyond the final, most visible run. Developers conduct experiments, test different designs, prepare datasets, fine tune models and sometimes repeat unsuccessful runs. Measuring only the final training operation can therefore understate the electricity used throughout development. Epoch AI's 2025 analysis suggested that training, experiments and inference each accounted for substantial shares of AI computing power demand, with no single activity necessarily dominating across the entire industry. These are estimates rather than a comprehensive measurement of every AI operator. epochai.substack.com Inference: smaller tasks, repeated continuously Inference begins when someone uses a trained model. Asking a chatbot a question, generating an image, translating a document or requesting a prediction all require the system to perform new calculations. Unlike training, which adjusts a model's parameters, inference generally uses parameters that have already been learned. A single inference request may be relatively inexpensive in electricity terms. But a successful AI service can receive requests continuously from individuals, businesses and automated software. The basic relationship is straightforward: Total inference electricity = electricity per request × number of requests. For example, if an AI service consumed an average of 0.3 watt hours (Wh) per request and processed one billion requests daily, it would use approximately 300 megawatt hours (MWh) per day, or 109.5 GWh annually. That calculation is illustrative. Actual electricity use depends on the mixture of requests, hardware and supporting infrastructure. Inference can eventually consume more electricity than the original training run because the model may remain in use for years. However, this is not inevitable: a specialised model with relatively few users may never accumulate enough inference demand to exceed its development energy. The 2026 Stanford Institute for Human Centered Artificial Intelligence AI Index identifies inference as a growing share of AI's energy footprint and notes that widely deployed models can accumulate inference energy exceeding their one off training consumption within months. AI INDEX REPORT 2026 The implication is that reducing training energy alone cannot guarantee that an AI service has a small long term electricity footprint. Illustration 1 AI Training vs Inference Electricity Patterns How much electricity does an AI request actually use? There is no universal electricity figure for an AI query. A request to summarise a short paragraph is fundamentally different from one that processes a large document, performs extensive reasoning or generates video. Even apparently identical requests can consume different amounts of electricity depending on the model, hardware, operating conditions and number of users sharing the system. Nevertheless, recent research provides useful reference points. What recent measurements show In August 2025, Google published an assessment of the electricity consumed by its Gemini AI assistant. It reported that a median Gemini text prompt used approximately 0.24 Wh, including the active AI processors, supporting computing equipment, idle capacity and data centre overheads. The"},{"title":"Ireland s Power","url":"/ireland-s-power/","description":"Ireland's new grid connection rules require larger data centres to support electricity supply and renewables, raising questions about costs and reliability.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Grid Pressure","level":3,"text":"Ireland s Power Must Ireland's Data Centres Pay for Their Own Power? Grid Pressure Ireland s Power Ireland s Power Must Ireland's Data Centres Pay for Their Own Power? Ireland's new grid connection rules require larger data centres to support electricity supply and renewables, raising questions about costs and reliability. Ireland's new grid connection rules require larger data centres to support electricity supply and renewables, raising questions about costs and reliability. AI data centre electricity demand governance single policy policy intervention Why Ireland replaced its earlier data centre connection restrictions in 2025 What the new generation, storage and 80% renewable electricity requirements mean Who pays for additional power capacity and which grid problems remain unresolved ai data centre electricity demand grid pressure ireland s power Updated Introduction Ireland's new data centre electricity rules make developers responsible for bringing additional power capacity and supporting new renewable generation, but they do not require data centres to operate independently of the national grid or pay every cost their expansion creates. On 12 December 2025, Ireland's Commission for Regulation of Utilities (CRU) introduced a new connection policy requiring larger data centres to provide electricity generation or storage capacity matching their maximum grid demand. Facilities above a minimum size must also match at least 80% of their annual electricity consumption with additional renewable generation in Ireland, with six years to meet that target. The policy replaces an earlier, highly restrictive approach with a conditional route to new connections. It aims to protect electricity reliability while allowing further investment in cloud computing and AI infrastructure. The unresolved question is whether these requirements genuinely prevent costs from being passed to households, businesses and the environment. CRU What must data centres provide under the new rules? The most important distinction is between providing enough dependable electricity capacity and supporting enough renewable electricity over a year. Ireland's policy requires both, but they solve different problems. Requirement What developers must do What it means for costs Generation or storage Provide capacity capable of matching the site's maximum permitted grid import, subject to size thresholds Developers must finance or contract for additional electricity infrastructure 80% renewable electricity Support additional renewable generation in Ireland equivalent to at least 80% of annual electricity demand Creates demand for new wind and solar projects rather than relying solely on existing supplies Six year transition Deliver the renewable requirement within six years of the data centre receiving power Gives developers time to build projects, but permits an initial period before full compliance Grid location assessment Demonstrate that the proposed connection can be accommodated without unacceptable network constraints A developer may still face refusal, delays or expensive infrastructure requirements Wholesale market participation Make qualifying generation or storage available through Ireland's electricity market Additional capacity can support the wider system and potentially earn revenue The policy applies specifically to data centres rather than automatically imposing identical obligations on all large industrial electricity users. The detailed requirements depend on a facility's maximum import capacity (MIC), meaning the maximum electricity it is contractually permitted to draw from the grid. Arthur Cox LLP Different rules for different sizes Ireland uses megavolt amperes (MVA) to measure connection capacity. This is closely related to megawatts (MW), although the two are not always numerically identical. Below 1 MVA: The principal generation and renewable electricity obligations do not apply, although local network constraints still matter. From 1 MVA to below 10 MVA: The developer must provide an electricity producing unit, known as an autoproducer, capable of covering the site's maximum import capacity on a reliability adjusted basis. It must participate in the wholesale market. At least 10 MVA: The developer must provide on site or nearby dispatchable generation and/or storage matching the site's maximum import capacity, after adjustments for how reliably that equipment can deliver electricity. This equipment must be separately connected and metered and participate in the electricity market. These distinctions are important because the largest facilities, including many hyperscale cloud and AI data centres, fall into the most demanding category. The generation requirement also applies as a facility expands. A large data centre cannot simply increase its electricity imports to full planned capacity before the corresponding qualifying generation or storage is available. Arthur Cox LLP Why Ireland replaced its earlier restrictions Ireland's December 2025 decision followed several years in which the country's data centre industry grew faster than the electricity system could comfortably accommodate in its most popular locations. In November 2021, the CRU introduced restrictions requiring electricity system operators to assess proposed data centre connections against network constraints, generation availability and the potential contribution of on site generation. Although these rules were not a formal nationwide prohibition, they made new connections particularly difficult around Dublin. Industry representatives described the resulting situation as a de facto moratorium. The December 2025 decision superseded the earlier direction for new applications and established clearer conditions under which developers could seek connections. Rather than relying primarily on restrictive case by case assessments, the new framework requires applicants to arrive with plans for supporting generation, renewable electricity and grid compatibility. CRU The electricity demand behind the decision The CRU reported that data centres accounted for 22% of Ireland's national electricity demand in 2024, up from 5% in 2015. The regulator also cited EirGrid forecasts suggesting that, under the assumptions used at the time, data centre electricity demand could rise from 9.4 terawatt hours (TWh) in 2025 to 14.6 TWh in 2034, reaching approximately 31% of national electricity demand. These are projections, not guaranteed outcomes. Actual demand will depend on connection approvals, investment decisions, computing efficiency and the pace of infrastructure construction. CRU The scale nevertheless explains the regulatory intervention. A rapidly expanding sector consuming a substantial share of national electricity cannot be treated as a collection of ordinary commercial connections. The problem is particularly pronounced around Dublin, where major technology companies have concentrated data centres to benefit from established fibre networks, cloud infrastructure and existing operations. Ireland therefore faced a difficult choice: continue restricting new connections, potentially discouraging investment, or permit further development while requiring operators to contribute more directly to electricity supply. The 2025 policy represents the second approach. Does the generation requirement mean data centres must produce all their own electricity? No. The policy requires qualifying generation or storage capacity to be available, not that every unit of electricity consumed by a data centre must be produced on its premises. This distinction is central to understanding both the benefits and limitations of Ireland's approach. Large data centres must provide dispatchable capacity. Dispatchable means electricity generation or storage that can be called upon when the system needs it, rather than relying entirely on weather conditions. Qualifying equipment can be located at the data centre or sufficiently close to it electrically. For larger facilities, it must participate in the wholesale electricity market, allowing its output to contribute to the wider electricity system rather than being reserved exclusively for the data centre. The developer can build the equipment directly or contract with another provider. Arthur Cox LLP Illustration 1 Ireland Data Centre Connection Process Why the amount of dependable power matters Consider a hypothetical data centre with a maximum grid import capacity of 100 MW, assuming for simplicity that its power factor makes 100 MW approximately equivalent to its relevant connection capacity. Under the new framework, it would need qualifying generation or storage with a reliability adjusted capacity matching that maximum import. But installing equipment labelled as 100 MW does not necessarily satisfy the requirement. The regulator uses de rating, which reduces the amount of capacity credited to equipment according to how reliably it can supply electricity when needed. For"},{"title":"Liquid Cooling Costs","url":"/liquid-cooling-costs/","description":"Cooling AI processors with liquid can cut refrigeration costs, but pumps, pipework and higher server densities complicate the savings.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Cooling Costs","level":3,"text":"Liquid Cooling Costs Does Liquid Cooling Really Make AI Data Centres Cheaper? Cooling Costs Liquid Cooling Costs Liquid Cooling Costs Does Liquid Cooling Really Make AI Data Centres Cheaper? Cooling AI processors with liquid can cut refrigeration costs, but pumps, pipework and higher server densities complicate the savings. Cooling AI processors with liquid can cut refrigeration costs, but pumps, pipework and higher server densities complicate the savings. AI data centre electricity demand implementation single mechanism mechanism How direct to chip cooling removes heat from powerful AI processors Electricity savings versus installation, pumping and maintenance costs When higher rack density outweighs the benefits of efficient cooling ai data centre electricity demand cooling costs liquid cooling costs Updated Introduction Direct to chip liquid cooling can reduce the electricity needed to cool powerful AI servers, but it does not automatically make an AI data centre cheaper to build or operate. The strongest financial case is usually found in new, densely packed AI facilities where conventional air cooling is becoming impractical. Retrofitting an existing data centre is more complicated: savings on refrigeration and fans must outweigh new spending on pumps, pipework, cooling equipment, installation and maintenance. Research published in 2026 suggests that converting from air cooling to liquid to chip cooling could reduce annual electricity consumption by approximately 4–13% per unit of computing work under the conditions modelled. That is a meaningful improvement, but not a universal saving. ScienceDirect The central distinction is between three different outcomes: lower cooling costs , lower electricity consumption per unit of AI computing, and lower total electricity demand. Liquid cooling can deliver the first two while a growing AI data centre still consumes more electricity overall. For operators comparing their options, the practical differences are: Decision factor Conventional air cooling Direct to chip liquid cooling Initial investment Usually lower where suitable air cooling infrastructure already exists Higher where new liquid infrastructure is required Cooling electricity More fan power and potentially more refrigeration Less airflow and potential for reduced refrigeration, offset by pumping High density AI racks Increasingly difficult at very high heat loads Better suited to concentrated processor heat Maintenance Established servicing practices Additional fluid management, connections and leak controls Retrofit complexity Often simpler for existing air cooled servers Depends on server compatibility and building infrastructure Long term economics Attractive for lower density equipment Stronger where high density computing justifies the investment The comparison is not simply between two machines that perform the same job. Direct to chip cooling can enable AI hardware configurations that would be difficult to operate with air cooling alone. That makes its value partly an electricity saving question and partly a question of whether the data centre can accommodate the computing equipment it intends to buy. How direct to chip cooling removes heat from AI processors Direct to chip cooling transfers heat from processors into a circulating liquid rather than relying mainly on air moving through the server. A metal component called a cold plate sits against a heat producing chip, typically a graphics processing unit (GPU) or central processing unit (CPU). Liquid flows through small channels inside the plate, absorbs heat and carries it away through pipes. The heat is then transferred into a wider cooling system and eventually released outside the building. The principle is straightforward: liquid can carry substantial quantities of heat through relatively small pipes, making it better suited than air to removing intense, concentrated heat loads. The equipment behind the cooling loop A typical installation includes several interconnected components. 1. Cold plates: Transfer heat directly from processors into the coolant. 2. Server manifolds and connections: Distribute coolant to individual components and collect the warmed liquid. 3. Coolant distribution units (CDUs): Control coolant flow, temperature and pressure, often separating the server circuit from the building's cooling water circuit. 4. Pumps, valves and sensors: Maintain circulation and monitor operating conditions. 5. Heat rejection equipment: Transfers the collected heat to outdoor air or water through dry coolers, cooling towers or other systems. These components explain why liquid cooling is not simply a matter of replacing server fans with water pipes. The American Society of Heating, Refrigerating and Air Conditioning Engineers (ASHRAE) describes the need to manage coolant temperatures, fluid quality, pressure, condensation and redundancy. A poorly controlled system can create reliability problems even if its heat transfer performance is excellent. ASHRAE Handbook Direct to chip systems also rarely eliminate air cooling completely. Memory, networking equipment, power supplies and other components may still release heat into the server room. Consequently, a facility often needs to operate liquid and air cooling together. How much electricity it saves depends partly on how much heat the liquid actually captures. Why warmer coolant can save electricity One of the most important benefits is not merely that liquid absorbs heat effectively. It is that processors can sometimes be cooled using water at higher temperatures than conventional chilled water systems require. Traditional refrigeration uses electricity to maintain sufficiently low temperatures. If a direct to chip system can operate with warmer water, the facility may be able to reduce compressor operation or bypass mechanical refrigeration during suitable weather. This is commonly called free cooling, although pumps, fans and other equipment still consume electricity. Research by Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory demonstrated the potential in 2014. Its direct liquid cooling study used 38 modified servers and two comparable unmodified servers, then modelled facility wide performance. The researchers estimated that the specified retrofit could produce approximately 20% overall data centre energy savings. That figure was conditional on the cooling system configurations modelled, rather than a measured 20% saving across an entire commercial AI data centre. Building & Industrial Energy Systems Division The distinction matters because the largest gains may come from avoiding refrigeration, not simply from replacing airflow with liquid circulation. Illustration 1 Direct to Chip Heat Removal Process Electricity savings versus installation and maintenance costs The strongest argument for direct to chip cooling is that it can reduce several sources of electricity consumption at once. The strongest objection is that obtaining those savings requires an additional engineered system. Where the electricity savings come from Liquid cooling can reduce electricity consumption through three main mechanisms. Lower server fan power. When cold plates remove a large share of processor heat, server fans may operate more slowly. Fan power can be particularly significant in tightly packed servers requiring high airflow. Reduced refrigeration. Warmer coolant can increase the number of hours when outdoor conditions allow heat to be rejected without compressor based chilling. Less room air cooling. Transferring processor heat directly into liquid reduces the quantity of hot air that must be collected and cooled inside the data hall. However, these savings must be calculated after allowing for CDU pumps, facility water pumps, outdoor cooling fans and any remaining air conditioning equipment. A 2026 study in Advances in Applied Energy, examining liquid to chip cooling with differential temperature control, estimated annual energy improvements of 4–13% per unit of computing work and peak demand improvements of 6–14% under its analysed configurations. It also found that higher coolant temperatures could reduce or eliminate the need for compressor based chilling in some Australian and Asian climate conditions. ScienceDirect The results support a genuine energy efficiency opportunity. They do not establish that every installation will save the same percentage, particularly where an existing facility already uses efficient air cooling and outdoor air economisers. What the savings mean in electricity bills Consider a hypothetical AI installation consuming 10 megawatts (MW) of computing equipment power continuously. Assume its total facility electricity consumption, including cooling and electrical overheads, is initially 12 MW. That corresponds to a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.20. PUE is the ratio of total data centre electricity consumption to the electricity consumed by computing equipment. A lower value generally indicates"},{"title":"Dublin Grid Pressure","url":"/dublin-grid-pressure/","description":"Dublin's concentration of data centres is creating electricity bottlenecks that additional power generation elsewhere in Ireland cannot solve alone.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Grid Pressure","level":3,"text":"Dublin Grid Pressure Why Dublin's Data Centres Are Straining the Local Grid Grid Pressure Dublin Grid Pressure Dublin Grid Pressure Why Dublin's Data Centres Are Straining the Local Grid Dublin's concentration of data centres is creating electricity bottlenecks that additional power generation elsewhere in Ireland cannot solve alone. Dublin's concentration of data centres is creating electricity bottlenecks that additional power generation elsewhere in Ireland cannot solve alone. AI data centre electricity demand mechanism single case aspect entity Why so many data centres were built around Dublin How substations and transmission bottlenecks restrict electricity connections What grid congestion means for new data centres and other electricity users ai data centre electricity demand grid pressure dublin grid pressure Updated Introduction Dublin's data centre boom has created one of Europe's clearest examples of electricity demand growing faster than the local grid can accommodate. Large computing facilities have clustered around the Irish capital, particularly in west Dublin, where they depend on the same high voltage transmission infrastructure and substations used to supply other businesses and communities. The pressure became serious enough for Ireland's electricity regulator to introduce restrictions on new data centre connections in November 2021. The problem was not simply a national shortage of electricity: it was also the difficulty of delivering enough power into the Greater Dublin area without overloading critical network infrastructure. By 2025, data centres accounted for 23% of Ireland's metered electricity consumption, up from 5% in 2015. These are national figures, not Dublin specific measurements, but they illustrate the scale of the demand growth behind the capital's grid constraints. Houses of the Oireachtas Dublin's experience reveals an important limitation of expanding electricity generation: building more power stations or wind farms elsewhere does not automatically create additional capacity in the substations and transmission lines serving a concentrated group of large electricity users. Why so many data centres were built around Dublin Dublin became a major European data centre hub because several commercial and technical advantages reinforced one another. Ireland's position as an English speaking member of the European Union, its established technology sector and its corporate tax environment helped attract international cloud companies. Dublin also offered access to international telecommunications networks, including submarine cables connecting Ireland with Britain, Europe and North America. For operators, concentrating facilities in an established technology hub offers practical advantages. Existing fibre optic connections can be extended, specialist contractors are already familiar with the industry, and neighbouring facilities can share access to suppliers, maintenance services and other infrastructure. These advantages encouraged further development around established industrial locations, particularly west and south west Dublin. The resulting concentration is visible in areas such as Grange Castle in Clondalkin and nearby parts of the Dublin industrial corridor. These locations became attractive to large cloud operators and specialist data centre developers because of their industrial land, digital connectivity and proximity to existing infrastructure. However, the same concentration that benefits the computing industry creates a problem for the electricity system. A single data centre can require tens of megawatts of electricity. A campus containing multiple buildings may need substantially more. When several operators seek large connections within the same geographical area, their combined demand can exceed the spare capacity available in nearby substations and transmission circuits. Unlike an ordinary office development, a large data centre may maintain a substantial electrical load throughout the day and night. Servers, networking equipment, cooling systems and supporting infrastructure must operate continuously. The result is a concentrated, relatively persistent demand for electricity that can leave little spare capacity for additional large connections. Ireland's Central Statistics Office (CSO) recorded data centre electricity consumption rising from 1,240 gigawatt hours (GWh) in 2015 to 7,663 GWh in 2025. This represents more than a sixfold increase over a decade. Central Statistics Office The figures also show why Dublin's infrastructure difficulties cannot be understood solely through national electricity demand. A substantial part of this growth occurred in a sector whose facilities were geographically concentrated, while the electricity network serving those locations could not expand at the same speed. How transmission lines and substations became bottlenecks Dublin's electricity problem involves a distinction between having enough electricity available somewhere on the system and having sufficient network capacity to deliver it to a particular site. Ireland's transmission network, operated by EirGrid, carries electricity at high voltages across the country. Substations connect different parts of the system, transform electricity between voltage levels and distribute power towards customers. Large data centres may connect directly to the transmission system or through infrastructure operated by ESB Networks, Ireland's distribution network operator. Both arrangements depend on the physical capacity of the surrounding electricity network. Illustration 1 Dublin Data Centre Connection Evolution Why additional generation elsewhere cannot solve the problem alone Consider a hypothetical data centre campus in west Dublin requesting a new 100 megawatt (MW) connection. Even if an additional 100 MW of generation becomes available elsewhere in Ireland, the new campus cannot necessarily use it. Electricity must pass through transmission circuits, substations and transformers capable of carrying the additional load. If a critical transformer is already approaching its permitted operating limit, adding more generation upstream does not increase that transformer's capacity. Similarly, if a transmission line into Dublin is constrained, electricity generated in another region may be unable to reach the proposed facility without exceeding safe operating limits. Electricity networks must also remain secure when equipment unexpectedly fails. A connection that appears technically possible under normal conditions may be unacceptable if the loss of a single major line or transformer would overload the remaining infrastructure. This is one reason network operators cannot simply allocate every available megawatt of theoretical capacity to new customers. The constraint can therefore arise at several points: Transmission circuits: High voltage lines may lack sufficient spare capacity to carry additional electricity into a heavily developed area. Substations: Transformers and switching equipment may be unable to accommodate another large customer without substantial reinforcement. Security requirements: Network operators must preserve sufficient operating margins to manage equipment failures and other disturbances. Construction lead times: New substations, transmission routes and transformers can require lengthy planning, procurement, approval and construction processes. These limitations explain why an electricity connection is not simply a commercial agreement to purchase enough power. It is also a technical commitment by the network operator to deliver that power safely and reliably. Why Dublin's problem became a regulatory issue By 2021, EirGrid had warned that continued data centre growth was creating significant network and electricity security concerns. On 23 November 2021, the Commission for Regulation of Utilities (CRU) issued a direction changing how new data centre connection applications would be assessed. The regulator required network operators to consider whether a proposed data centre was located in a constrained area, whether it could provide suitable on site generation or storage, and whether it could reduce its electricity consumption when instructed. The Greater Dublin region was subsequently identified as an area with regional transmission constraints. Houses of the Oireachtas This marked an important change in how Ireland approached large computing facilities. Previously, the main commercial question for developers was often how quickly they could obtain a suitable connection and bring a facility into operation. Under the revised approach, the location and flexibility of the proposed electricity demand became central considerations. A developer offering to pay for electricity was no longer sufficient evidence that the grid could accommodate the project. The network also had to be capable of supporting the facility without creating unacceptable risks for the wider system. The Castlebaggot"},{"title":"Water vs Power","url":"/water-vs-power/","description":"Using less water to cool AI data centres can increase electricity demand, especially when dry cooling systems face high outdoor temperatures.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Cooling Costs","level":3,"text":"Water vs Power Can AI Data Centres Save Water Without Using More Electricity? Cooling Costs Water vs Power Water vs Power Can AI Data Centres Save Water Without Using More Electricity? Using less water to cool AI data centres can increase electricity demand, especially when dry cooling systems face high outdoor temperatures. Using less water to cool AI data centres can increase electricity demand, especially when dry cooling systems face high outdoor temperatures. AI data centre electricity demand critique risk bounded topic mechanism Why evaporative cooling saves electricity but consumes water How dry and closed loop heat rejection perform during hot weather Choosing cooling systems when local water supplies and electricity costs are constrained ai data centre electricity demand cooling costs water vs power Updated Introduction AI data centres can save substantial amounts of water by switching from evaporative cooling to dry cooling, but this often increases electricity demand. The trade off becomes particularly difficult in hot, water stressed regions, where dry cooling equipment must work hardest precisely when electricity grids may already be under pressure. There is no universally superior cooling system. Evaporative cooling generally uses less electricity to remove heat but consumes water. Dry cooling greatly reduces direct water consumption, yet can require more fan power, mechanical refrigeration and electrical infrastructure. Hybrid systems can balance the two, although they may still consume water during the hottest periods. Research comparing two data centres in Phoenix, Arizona, found that the air cooled facility had nearly 13% higher power usage effectiveness (PUE), indicating greater electricity overhead, but 66% lower source water usage effectiveness (WUE), which includes water associated with electricity generation. ScienceDirect For water stressed communities, the central question is therefore not simply whether a data centre uses water. It is whether the electricity saved through evaporation justifies consuming a scarce local resource, and whether avoiding that consumption shifts environmental costs elsewhere. Which cooling system makes the most sense? The practical choice depends on local water availability, outdoor temperatures, electricity costs and the reliability requirements of the data centre. Cooling approach Electricity demand Direct water consumption Main consideration Evaporative cooling Generally lower Moderate to high Efficient heat removal, but depends on reliable water supplies Dry cooling Often higher, particularly in hot weather Very low or zero for heat rejection Conserves local water but increases electrical cooling requirements Closed loop liquid cooling with dry heat rejection Potentially efficient, depending on coolant temperatures Very low during normal operation Reduces water losses but still requires outdoor heat rejection Hybrid dry and evaporative cooling Between the alternatives, depending on operation Variable Can minimise annual water use while preserving cooling capacity during heatwaves These are engineering tendencies rather than fixed performance rankings. A well designed dry system in a favourable climate can outperform a poorly operated evaporative system. The distinction between liquid cooling inside a data centre and water consumption outside it is especially important. Liquid circulating through AI servers does not necessarily get consumed. The question is how that liquid eventually releases its heat to the environment. The US Department of Energy and Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory identify both cooling technology and local climate as major determinants of electricity and water efficiency. Department of Energy For decision makers, three principles follow. Where water supplies are severely constrained, dry cooling deserves strong consideration, even if its electricity requirements are somewhat higher. Where electricity capacity is the main bottleneck and water is sustainably available, evaporative cooling may provide a better overall result. Where both resources are constrained, hybrid operation, higher temperature liquid cooling and careful site selection may be more effective than choosing either extreme. The rest of the decision depends on understanding where the water and electricity actually go. Why evaporative cooling saves electricity but consumes water Evaporative cooling exploits a simple physical process: water absorbs heat when it changes from liquid into vapour. The same principle explains why sweating cools the human body. A data centre can use it to transfer heat into the atmosphere without relying as heavily on energy intensive refrigeration. In a conventional water cooled installation, heat passes from servers into a cooling system and eventually reaches an outdoor cooling tower. Some water evaporates, carrying heat away. Replacement water must continually enter the system. This allows cooling equipment to operate at temperatures that would be difficult to achieve using outdoor air alone. The advantage of the wet bulb temperature Two outdoor temperature measurements help explain the difference. Dry bulb temperature is the ordinary air temperature reported in weather forecasts. Wet bulb temperature reflects the cooling achievable through evaporation. It depends on both temperature and humidity. In hot, dry conditions, wet bulb temperature can be substantially lower than dry bulb temperature. An evaporative system can exploit this difference to reject heat more effectively. A dry cooling system cannot obtain the same evaporative advantage. Its performance is largely constrained by the temperature of the surrounding air. This explains why evaporative cooling can be attractive in desert regions despite their limited water supplies: the dry climate makes evaporation particularly effective. The US National Renewable Energy Laboratory identifies lower cooling temperatures, reduced airflow requirements and improved energy efficiency as important advantages of water cooled heat rejection, while emphasising its dependence on a continuous water supply. NREL Where the water actually goes Evaporative systems circulate much more water than they consume. Nevertheless, the losses are unavoidable if evaporation is the principal cooling mechanism. Water leaves the system through evaporation, small amounts of escaping droplets and the controlled discharge of mineral rich water. The last process is called blowdown. As water evaporates, dissolved minerals remain behind. Operators must periodically remove some concentrated water to prevent scaling, corrosion and operational problems. The distinction matters because water withdrawal and water consumption are not identical. Water that evaporates is consumed from the immediate local water system. Water discharged through blowdown may enter wastewater treatment and potentially return to the wider water system, although its availability and quality can change. The US Department of Energy explains that increasing a cooling tower's cycles of concentration from three to six can reduce replacement water requirements by approximately 20% and blowdown by 50%. This improves water efficiency without eliminating the fundamental evaporation requirement. Department of Energy For operators, better water treatment and monitoring can therefore reduce consumption and operating costs. But even an efficiently managed evaporative system continues to depend on water availability. Why low electricity use is not the whole environmental answer An evaporative system may achieve an excellent electricity efficiency rating while drawing heavily on municipal water, groundwater or other local sources. That distinction becomes politically important when data centres compete with households, agriculture, industry and ecosystems for limited supplies. A litre of water consumed in a relatively water rich region does not necessarily carry the same social or environmental cost as a litre consumed in an overallocated desert basin. Consequently, the electricity savings from evaporative cooling cannot be evaluated independently of local water conditions. A 2025 Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory review found that water consumption per unit of data centre workload varies enormously, with cooling technology, electricity supply, server efficiency and location all influencing the outcome. The researchers concluded that there is no single configuration that minimises water use everywhere. Energy Technologies Area Illustration 1 Cooling System Trade offs in Data Centres How dry cooling performs during hot weather Dry cooling removes heat without deliberately evaporating water into the atmosphere. Typically, fans move outdoor air across heat exchangers containing a circulating fluid. Heat passes into the air and is released outdoors. Some systems use mechanical chillers to maintain sufficiently low temperatures. Others can reject heat directly when outdoor conditions"},{"title":"Household Grid Costs","url":"/household-grid-costs/","description":"New grid infrastructure for AI data centres can raise household electricity bills when upgrade costs are shared across customers.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Who Pays","level":3,"text":"Household Grid Costs Are Households Paying for AI Data Centre Grid Upgrades? Who Pays Household Grid Costs Household Grid Costs Are Households Paying for AI Data Centre Grid Upgrades? New grid infrastructure for AI data centres can raise household electricity bills when upgrade costs are shared across customers. New grid infrastructure for AI data centres can raise household electricity bills when upgrade costs are shared across customers. AI data centre electricity demand mechanism single mechanism mechanism How data centre connections trigger wider grid upgrades How Ofgem's network charges spread infrastructure costs across electricity bills When shared grid investment benefits households and when costs are unfairly shifted ai data centre electricity demand who pays household grid costs Updated Introduction Yes, households can end up paying part of the cost of electricity grid upgrades needed by AI data centres. This happens when new substations, cables and wider network reinforcements are funded through charges shared across electricity customers, rather than paid entirely by the companies creating the additional demand. In Great Britain, data centre operators generally pay for their own connections and contribute to ongoing network charges. However, some upgrades to the shared electricity network are recovered through regulated charges that also appear on household bills. The distinction matters because a single large data centre can require substantial new grid capacity, while the infrastructure may be paid for over decades. There is no reliable published figure showing how much of the average UK household electricity bill is specifically attributable to AI data centre grid upgrades. In September 2026, the government confirmed that it was not tracking network reinforcement costs associated specifically with new data centres. UK Parliament How data centre connections trigger wider grid upgrades A data centre does not necessarily need an entirely new electricity network. If sufficient spare capacity exists, connecting it may involve relatively limited work. But a large facility operating powerful AI chips around the clock can require hundreds of megawatts of reliable electricity capacity. Where the existing grid cannot accommodate that demand, network operators may need to install larger transformers, reinforce substations, upgrade high voltage cables or strengthen transmission routes serving the wider area. The important distinction is between two types of infrastructure: Dedicated connection assets: Equipment built specifically to connect the data centre, such as a cable running from an existing network connection point to the facility. Shared network reinforcement: Upgrades to infrastructure that serves multiple customers, such as increasing the capacity of an existing substation or strengthening an electricity transmission route. The first category is generally charged directly to the connecting customer. The second can be recovered from a wider group of electricity users, depending on the applicable charging rules. Ofgem's 2022 Access Significant Code Review explicitly distinguishes between these costs. It introduced a system under which customers connecting to electricity distribution networks generally pay for their dedicated connection assets but do not pay ordinary reinforcement charges, subject to safeguards for unusually expensive connections. Final Decision Illustration 1 How Data Centre Demand Reaches Household Bills Why the location of a data centre matters Consider two hypothetical AI data centres, each requiring 200 megawatts of power. [parallelparliament.co.uk] parallelparliament.co.uk Q. Electricity: Data Centres Q. Electricity: Data Centres The first is built near a major substation with spare capacity. Its connection might require dedicated equipment but relatively little additional reinforcement. The second is built in an area where existing transmission lines and substations are already operating close to their limits. Serving it could require upgrades extending well beyond the site itself. Although both facilities consume the same amount of electricity, the second may impose substantially higher infrastructure costs. This is why the geographical concentration of data centres matters. Clusters of large facilities can create investment requirements that would not arise from the same electricity demand spread across locations with available grid capacity. Britain's National Energy System Operator (NESO) uses geographically differentiated transmission charges partly to reflect these differences in network costs. However, the charges do not necessarily assign every pound of new infrastructure expenditure directly to the individual project that triggered it. National Energy System Operator How Ofgem's network charges reach household bills Households do not usually receive a separate charge labelled'AI data centre infrastructure'. Instead, shared network costs become part of the electricity prices charged by suppliers. Ofgem regulates how much electricity network companies can recover through its RIIO price control framework. These regulated revenues pay for operating, maintaining and upgrading the networks, including a return on approved investment. The costs then flow through two main charging systems. Transmission Network Use of System (TNUoS) charges recover the cost of the high voltage electricity network carrying power across Great Britain. NESO collects these charges from generators, electricity suppliers and certain directly connected electricity users. Distribution Use of System (DUoS) charges recover the cost of regional and local electricity networks. These are charged to suppliers and other eligible network users, with costs ultimately reflected in electricity tariffs. Both mechanisms can distribute infrastructure expenditure across many customers rather than assigning it entirely to one development. National Energy System Operator How much of a household bill already pays for networks? The House of Commons Library's May 2026 analysis provides a useful benchmark. NETWORK INFRASTRUCTURE CHARGES, APRIL–JUNE 2026 per year for typical electricity use About 23% of the typical electricity price cap bill, according to the Commons Library. This covers network infrastructure generally, not data centres specifically. House of Commons Library These charges are recovered through a combination of electricity unit rates and daily standing charges. Consequently, households can contribute towards infrastructure costs even when their own electricity consumption has not increased. The transmission element is particularly relevant. The Commons Library estimated that transmission network charges would add approximately £83 to a typical annual domestic electricity bill in 2026/27, compared with £51 in 2025/26. Most of this transmission cost is recovered through the standing charge. Research Briefings That increase should not be described as an AI surcharge. Transmission investment is also needed to connect renewable generation, replace ageing equipment, improve reliability and reduce congestion. Published national bill figures do not isolate the proportion caused by data centres. Ofgem's December 2025 investment decisions illustrate the wider trade off. The regulator said that prospective electricity transmission investment could contribute to network charges rising by around £108 annually for a median use household by 2031, if all the prospective investment proceeded. It also projected savings from a more efficient electricity system, including reduced costs from network congestion. These were system wide estimates, not data centre specific forecasts. Ofgem Illustration 2 Dedicated Assets vs Shared Network Reinforcement When do data centres pay, and when are costs shared? The allocation of costs depends on where a data centre connects and which infrastructure needs upgrading. For distribution network connections, reforms introduced in April 2023 moved towards a'fully shallow'charging model. In plain English, this means connecting electricity users generally pay for their own connection equipment, while ordinary upgrades to the existing shared distribution network are funded through network charges. There is an important exception. Ofgem introduced a demand high cost threshold of £1,720 per kilovolt ampere of connection capacity, designed to prevent exceptionally expensive connections from imposing disproportionate reinforcement costs on other customers. The detailed charging rules determine which costs fall above this threshold. Final Decision For transmission connected data centres, separate connection and network use charging arrangements apply. Large users can face substantial ongoing charges reflecting their demand and location. They are therefore not exempt from paying for the network simply because some investment is shared. The financial question is whether those charges adequately reflect the additional"},{"title":"Query Electricity","url":"/query-electricity/","description":"Ordinary AI text queries can use less than one watt-hour, but complex reasoning, supporting equipment and billions of requests change the total.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / AI Energy Use","level":3,"text":"Query Electricity How Much Electricity Does One AI Chatbot Question Use? AI Energy Use Query Electricity Query Electricity How Much Electricity Does One AI Chatbot Question Use? Ordinary AI text queries can use less than one watt hour, but complex reasoning, supporting equipment and billions of requests change the total. Ordinary AI text queries can use less than one watt hour, but complex reasoning, supporting equipment and billions of requests change the total. AI data centre electricity demand evidence bounded topic dataset evidence What Google's 0.24 Wh measurement and independent 2026 estimates reveal Why short text prompts and demanding reasoning tasks consume different amounts of electricity How measurement boundaries and billions of daily requests change the overall electricity picture ai data centre electricity demand ai energy use query electricity Updated Introduction A typical short AI chatbot question can use around 0.2–0.3 watt hours (Wh) of electricity, according to recent measurements and research estimates. That is roughly enough electricity to run a 10 watt LED light bulb for one to two minutes. More demanding questions, particularly those involving extended reasoning, lengthy documents or multiple AI operations, can consume several watt hours or considerably more. Google reported 0.24 Wh for the median text prompt sent to its Gemini assistant in May 2025. A peer reviewed study published in Joule in April 2026 independently estimated a median of 0.31 Wh for standard queries handled by efficiently operated, large AI models. Google Services These figures challenge older claims that every chatbot question requires 3 Wh or more. But they do not mean AI electricity demand is negligible. The amount used by an individual question depends on the model, the length of the answer, the hardware and the supporting data centre equipment. When billions of requests are processed, even small amounts add up. How much electricity does a typical AI question use? Electricity consumption is measured in watt hours (Wh). One watt hour is the electricity used by a one watt device running for an hour. One kilowatt hour (kWh), the unit commonly used on household electricity bills, equals 1,000 Wh. When an AI chatbot responds to a question, specialised computer chips perform calculations to generate the answer. This process is called inference. The electricity consumed depends partly on how much computing is required and partly on how efficiently the data centre handles the request. The following figures show why there is no single reliable electricity cost for every chatbot question. Source and scenario Electricity per query What the figure represents Google Gemini, May 2025 0.24 Wh Measured median text prompt, including allocated supporting equipment and data centre overhead Epoch AI, February 2025 Approximately 0.30 Wh Analytical estimate for a typical GPT 4o text query Joule, April 2026 0.31 Wh Modelled median for standard queries under optimised large scale serving conditions Joule, April 2026, extended reasoning 3.91 Wh Modelled median for substantially longer responses, around 5,000 output tokens Epoch AI, very long inputs Approximately 2.5–40 Wh Estimated GPT 4o consumption for prompts containing 10,000–100,000 input tokens Sources: Google's technical measurement study, Epoch AI's February 2025 analysis and Oviedo and colleagues'2026 Joule study. These are different workloads and methodologies, not directly interchangeable measurements. Google Services The most useful rule of thumb is that an ordinary, efficiently served text exchange may consume less than 1 Wh, while a complex exchange can require several times or tens of times more. These are estimates of electricity used to operate the AI service, rather than a complete accounting of every resource needed to create and maintain it. They should not be applied automatically to image generation, video production or other substantially different AI tasks. What Google's 0.24 Wh measurement reveals In August 2025, Google published a technical paper, Measuring the Environmental Impact of Delivering AI at Google Scale. It provided unusually detailed information about the electricity used to answer requests in its Gemini service. Google calculated that the median text prompt in May 2025 consumed 0.24 Wh. Crucially, this was not simply the electricity drawn by the main AI processing chip. The calculation also included several supporting sources of consumption. Google Services Electricity used by Google's median Gemini text prompt AI accelerator chips 0.14 Wh Processors and memory 0.06 Wh Idle machines 0.02 Wh Data centre overhead 0.02 Wh Total Google's fleet wide median, May 2025. Percentages are approximate and may differ slightly because of rounding. The distinction between chip electricity and total operating electricity is important. Google's narrower measurement approach produced a figure of 0.10 Wh, while its more comprehensive calculation produced 0.24 Wh. The two figures describe the same general type of activity but use different accounting boundaries. A narrow measurement can make AI appear substantially more efficient simply because it leaves out some supporting electricity consumption. Google also estimated that the median prompt generated 0.03 grams of carbon dioxide equivalent and consumed 0.26 millilitres of water under its comprehensive accounting approach. Those figures depend on the company's infrastructure, energy accounting and operating conditions; they should not be treated as universal values for all AI systems. Google Services Illustration 1 Google Gemini Electricity Accounting How reliable is Google's figure? Google's research has a significant advantage over estimates based only on published chip specifications: the company can observe its own production systems and their operating characteristics. Nevertheless, the 0.24 Wh result has limitations. First, it is a median, not an average. Half the measured prompts fall below the median and half above it. A relatively small number of extremely demanding requests could raise the average electricity consumption without substantially changing the median. Second, the measurement describes Gemini text prompts during a particular month. It does not establish how much electricity every Gemini model, enterprise application or future version consumes. Third, Google's measurement methodology is company developed, and outsiders do not have unrestricted access to the underlying production data. The detailed breakdown makes the calculation more informative, but it does not make the result independently reproducible in full. For these reasons, the figure is best understood as strong evidence that short, efficiently processed text prompts can be relatively inexpensive in electricity terms, rather than proof that every AI question uses approximately a quarter of a watt hour. What independent research found in 2026 The strongest recent comparison comes from research published in Joule in April 2026 by Felipe Oviedo and colleagues, including researchers at Microsoft. Their study, Energy Use of AI Inference, Efficiency Pathways, and Test Time Scaling, estimated electricity consumption using realistic assumptions about AI models, computer hardware, processing speed and data centre operation. The researchers examined large language models, including models with hundreds of billions of parameters. Parameters are numerical values learned during training that help determine how a model produces an answer. For standard queries, the study estimated a median electricity requirement of 0.31 Wh, with the middle half of modelled results falling between 0.16 and 0.60 Wh. ScienceDirect This is close to Google's measured 0.24 Wh and Epoch AI's earlier estimate of 0.30 Wh for GPT 4o. The agreement is meaningful because the estimates were developed using different approaches, although the research does not constitute a fully independent audit of commercial chatbot electricity consumption. The Joule study also identified a major reason why some earlier estimates were much higher: they did not adequately reflect how commercial AI systems share processing capacity between many requests. A data centre serving millions of customers can group requests together, distribute work efficiently and use specialised software to reduce unnecessary calculations. Testing one model on an underused machine can therefore produce a much higher electricity per query figure than operating the same type of model in a carefully optimised service. The researchers estimated that some previously published figures overstated electricity consumption under comparable large scale serving assumptions by factors of four to twenty. That does not mean every high estimate is wrong. It means measurements from different operating conditions cannot automatically be compared as though they represent the same service. ScienceDirect A useful independent check comes from the ML.ENERGY"},{"title":"Training Energy","url":"/training-energy/","description":"A major AI training run can consume tens of gigawatt-hours, while experiments, chip utilisation and cooling add to its electricity footprint.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / AI Energy Use","level":3,"text":"Training Energy How Much Power Does Training a Large AI Model Need? AI Energy Use Training Energy Training Energy How Much Power Does Training a Large AI Model Need? A major AI training run can consume tens of gigawatt hours, while experiments, chip utilisation and cooling add to its electricity footprint. A major AI training run can consume tens of gigawatt hours, while experiments, chip utilisation and cooling add to its electricity footprint. AI data centre electricity demand mechanism bounded topic mechanism Why thousands of AI chips require so much electricity during training What a 50 MW training run and the Gemini Ultra estimate reveal about power demand How experiments, training duration and data centre overheads increase total consumption ai data centre electricity demand ai energy use training energy Updated Introduction Training a large artificial intelligence (AI) model can consume tens or even hundreds of gigawatt hours (GWh) of electricity, depending on the number of chips, their power requirements and how long training continues. A gigawatt hour is one million kilowatt hours (kWh), the unit used on household electricity bills. For perspective, a training system drawing 50 megawatts (MW) continuously for 60 days would consume 72 GWh. That is enough electricity to supply approximately 20,000 UK households for a year, assuming annual consumption of 3,600 kWh each. The comparison illustrates the scale, rather than describing a particular AI model. The electricity does not simply power the AI processors. Servers, networking equipment, cooling systems and supporting infrastructure also consume energy. Experiments conducted before the final training run can increase the overall footprint further. Understanding these costs matters because training creates concentrated electricity demand that can influence data centre construction, grid connections, infrastructure investment and local electricity supplies. Why do thousands of AI chips consume so much electricity during training? Training a large AI model involves repeatedly processing enormous datasets and adjusting the numerical settings that determine how the model operates. These settings, known as parameters, can number in the billions or trillions. The work is divided between thousands of specialised processors, usually graphics processing units (GPUs) or tensor processing units (TPUs). These chips perform large numbers of mathematical calculations simultaneously, making them suitable for the intensive computing required by modern AI. Unlike an ordinary computer running occasional tasks, a large training cluster may operate continuously for weeks or months. Its processors exchange information through high speed networks while repeatedly calculating how the model should change. The electricity required depends on four main factors: Number of processors: Larger clusters generally require more electrical power because more chips and supporting equipment operate simultaneously. Power consumption per processor: Modern AI accelerators can consume hundreds of watts each, with some newer hardware requiring considerably more. Training duration: A cluster running for 100 days consumes substantially more electricity than the same cluster running for 20 days at the same average power. Hardware utilisation: Processors do not always operate at their maximum computational capacity. Communication delays, memory limitations and interruptions can extend training without producing equivalent useful progress. Research organisation Epoch AI found that the power requirements of leading AI training runs increased by approximately 2.1 times annually in its historical analysis published in 2024. The main driver was the growing number of processors used, rather than dramatic increases in the electricity consumption of individual chips. Epoch AI The difference between power and electricity consumption Power and electricity consumption are closely related but measure different things. Power, measured in watts or megawatts, describes how quickly electricity is being used at a particular moment. Electricity consumption, measured in kilowatt hours, megawatt hours or gigawatt hours, describes the total electricity used over time. The basic calculation is: Electricity (MWh) = Power (MW) × Hours \\text{Electricity (MWh)}=\\text{Power (MW)}\\times\\text{Hours} Electricity (MWh) = Power (MW) × Hours For example, a 10 MW training cluster operating continuously for 24 hours consumes 240 MWh. A 50 MW cluster running for the same period consumes 1,200 MWh, or 1.2 GWh. This distinction is important because a training run can have two separate consequences. Its total electricity consumption determines how much energy must be supplied, while its instantaneous power requirement determines how much electrical capacity must be available. A data centre might have sufficient electricity available over a year but still struggle to secure a grid connection capable of supplying a large training cluster at full power. What does a 50 MW training run actually consume? Consider a hypothetical AI training system drawing an average of 50 MW from its computing equipment. The longer it operates, the greater its electricity consumption. Training duration Electricity used at 50 MW 24 hours 1.2 GWh 7 days 8.4 GWh 30 days 36 GWh 60 days 72 GWh 90 days 108 GWh 120 days 144 GWh These figures assume a constant average computing load of 50 MW. They exclude additional electricity consumed by cooling and other data centre infrastructure. Electricity consumed by a 50 MW training workload Illustrative calculation at a constant 50 MW, excluding facility overheads. Illustration 1 AI Model Development Energy Lifecycle 0 GWh 40 GWh 80 GWh 120 GWh 160 GWh 0 7 30 60 90 120 0: Electricity consumption 0 GWh 7: Electricity consumption 8.4 GWh 30: Electricity consumption 36 GWh 60: Electricity consumption 72 GWh 90: Electricity consumption 108 GWh 120: Electricity consumption 144 GWh The relationship is straightforward: doubling training duration doubles electricity consumption if average power remains unchanged. But actual training runs are more complicated. Power consumption changes as processors perform different tasks, encounter communication bottlenecks or experience interruptions. A facility's maximum rated capacity is therefore not necessarily its average electricity demand. Estimating a training run yourself A useful first approximation requires three pieces of information: average power, training duration and the efficiency of the data centre. Training electricity calculator Illustrative Average IT power 50 MW Training duration 60 days Data centre efficiency (PUE) 1.10 1.20 1.40 Computing electricity Total facility electricity Assumes constant average IT power and the selected power usage effectiveness (PUE). The result excludes separate experiments, chip manufacturing and subsequent model use. PUE, or power usage effectiveness, measures the ratio of total data centre electricity consumption to the electricity consumed by computing equipment. A PUE of 1.20 means that every 1 kWh used by computing equipment is associated with 1.20 kWh of total facility electricity consumption. This approach is consistent with the broad methodology used by Epoch AI, which estimates training power from processor electricity requirements, server overheads and data centre efficiency. Epoch AI The main limitation is that published processor power ratings are not necessarily measured average consumption. A more reliable calculation uses actual electricity readings from the training system. What do real AI training runs reveal? Publicly available estimates show that training electricity consumption varies considerably between models. Older large language models consumed hundreds or thousands of megawatt hours. More recent frontier systems can require tens of gigawatt hours or more. However, disclosure remains uneven. Some developers publish processor hours or estimated emissions without providing independently verified electricity consumption. Model Training electricity Evidence GPT 3 (2020) Approximately 1.29 GWh Third party estimate BLOOM (2022) Approximately 0.43 GWh Developer associated research GPT 4 (2023) Approximately 42.4 GWh IEA estimate Llama 3.1 405B (2024) Approximately 21.6 GWh from GPU ratings alone Derived from developer reported GPU hours; excludes additional equipment Sources: research published in Joule, the BLOOM study, the International Energy Agency and Meta's Llama 3.1 model documentation. These estimates are not perfectly comparable because their boundaries and assumptions differ. DOI GPT 4: an estimated 42.4 GWh The International Energy Agency's 2025 Energy and AI report estimated that GPT 4's training equipment had a total rated power requirement of approximately 22 MW, including supporting equipment and cooling. The IEA estimated that training lasted around 14 weeks. Applying an assumed average load factor of 84%, it calculated electricity"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-data-centre-elect-13a926-index/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of AI data centre electricity demand, covering Cooling Costs, Who Pays and Grid Pressure and more.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth?","level":2,"text":"All Topics AI data centre electricity demand: All Topics All Topics All Topics AI data centre electricity demand: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of AI data centre electricity demand, covering Cooling Costs, Who Pays and Grid Pressure and more. AI data centre electricity demand Explore Topics in AI data centre electricity demand The following pages expand on the main AI data centre electricity demand page and cover its key branches in more detail. Cooling Costs Who Pays Grid Pressure AI Energy Use"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-data-centre-elect-13a926-ai-data/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of Cooling Costs, covering Liquid Cooling Costs and Water vs Power.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Cooling Costs","level":3,"text":"All Topics Cooling Costs: All Topics All Topics All Topics Cooling Costs: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of Cooling Costs, covering Liquid Cooling Costs and Water vs Power. AI data centre electricity demand Explore Topics in Cooling Costs The following pages expand on the main Cooling Costs page and cover its key branches in more detail. Liquid Cooling Costs Water vs Power"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-data-centre-elect-13a926-ai/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of Who Pays, covering Household Grid Costs.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Who Pays","level":3,"text":"All Topics Who Pays: All Topics All Topics All Topics Who Pays: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of Who Pays, covering Household Grid Costs. AI data centre electricity demand Explore Topics in Who Pays The following pages expand on the main Who Pays page and cover its key branches in more detail. Household Grid Costs"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-data-centre-elect-13a926-ai-0a08e0/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of Grid Pressure, covering Dublin Grid Pressure and Ireland s Power.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / Grid Pressure","level":3,"text":"All Topics Grid Pressure: All Topics All Topics All Topics Grid Pressure: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of Grid Pressure, covering Dublin Grid Pressure and Ireland s Power. AI data centre electricity demand Explore Topics in Grid Pressure The following pages expand on the main Grid Pressure page and cover its key branches in more detail. Dublin Grid Pressure Ireland s Power"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-data-centre-elect-13a926-ai-training/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of AI Energy Use, covering Query Electricity and Training Energy.","breadcrumb":"Can Power Grids Keep Up With AI Growth? / AI Energy Use","level":3,"text":"All Topics AI Energy Use: All Topics All Topics All Topics AI Energy Use: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of AI Energy Use, covering Query Electricity and Training Energy. AI data centre electricity demand Explore Topics in AI Energy Use The following pages expand on the main AI Energy Use page and cover its key branches in more detail. Query Electricity Training Energy"}]};
